<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The app's user-facing text.

  This file exists because the original had no string layer at all: every label,
  placeholder, empty state and error message in the SwiftUI sources is a hardcoded
  English literal, which `AGENTS.md` in that project records as a known gap. Porting
  the literals verbatim would carry the gap across, so the text is extracted here
  instead and every screen reads it through `Res.string`.

  It lives in :packages:design-system rather than in each feature because that is the
  only module allowed to configure `compose.resources`. Every module that configures
  it emits its own `*ResourceCollectors` class, and two of those fail Android's dex
  merge with "defined multiple times". A feature that needs a new string adds it here.

  Values are grouped by who owns the wording, not by screen, because the same string
  is read from more than one place and a per-screen grouping would invite a second
  copy. The parameters are positional (`%1$s`) so a translator can reorder them.

  Strings that are genuinely part of the wire contract - a status enum value, a deep
  link path segment - are NOT here. Those are code, and they live with the type that
  defines them.

  READING ONE. A generated accessor is an *extension property* on `Res.string`, not a
  member of it, so it needs its own import:

      import com.tasktracker.resources.status_backlog
      ...
      ttText(text = stringResource(Res.string.status_backlog))

  Two of the three imports above are the non-obvious half. `Res` alone does not bring
  the string names into scope, and the error for a forgotten one is
  "Unresolved reference" on the *name* while `Res` and `stringResource` both resolve -
  which reads like a generation failure rather than a missing import. The `Res` class is
  generated even when the file above is empty, so a generated-but-empty `Res` is not
  evidence that the strings are missing.
-->
